integration_branch=orchid/integration
verify=npm test
# The repository's own floor, checked once per merge on the MERGED tree, in
# addition to whatever the task named for itself. Set it here and every task
# inherits it: unlike `verify` (which a task overrides with its own
# `verification_commands`), nothing a task writes can switch this off. That is
# the whole point -- a repo-wide check that each task has to opt into reaches
# only the tasks whose author remembered it. A failing gate sends the task to
# `rework` and leaves the integration ref exactly where it was. Unset (the
# default) means no gate and no cost.
#
# A red gate COSTS the task a rework round, and blocks it once the rounds run
# out (`rework_max`, or a per-task grant from `orchid task retry --attempts
# N`). Merge conflicts and a failing task suite still cost nothing at merge --
# this one failure does, because a gate is a statement about the repository
# and a repository nobody has touched is red again next round, so an uncharged
# edge would re-dispatch implementers against it forever. See
# docs/configuration.md for the recoveries when the gate was not the task's
# fault.
#
# Name the part of your checks that a task's own suite does NOT already
# cover -- lint, formatting, a licence scan. `orchid merge` has already run
# the task's `verification_commands` on this very tree by the time the gate
# fires, so a gate that repeats the test suite pays for it twice per merge for
# nothing. Orchid's own gate below is `scripts/ci-local.sh --no-tests` for
# exactly that reason: static checks and ShellCheck, no second suite. See
# docs/configuration.md.
# merge_gate=/bin/bash scripts/ci-local.sh --bash /bin/bash --no-tests

# A checkout Orchid creates -- a task's dispatch worktree, and the temp
# worktree `orchid merge` validates in -- is `git worktree add` of a ref, so
# it holds ONLY what is committed. If the `verify` command above needs
# anything untracked (installed dependencies, a generated file, a .env), set
# worktree_prepare to the command that puts it there; it runs once inside
# each fresh checkout, before anything else uses it, and is skipped on later
# passes until you change it. Unset (the default) means no preparation step
# at all -- correct for any project whose committed tree is enough to verify.
#
# The command's cwd is the checkout and its stdin is closed (it runs
# unattended -- there is nobody to answer a prompt), and it is given
# ORCHID_REPO_ROOT (this repository's own canonical path -- a dispatch
# worktree is a sibling of it, a merge worktree is somewhere under $TMPDIR,
# so nothing relative reaches it from both), ORCHID_WORKTREE and ORCHID_TASK
# (the bare task id in both checkouts). Output goes to
# .orchid/runtime/worktree-prepare/<task>.log, or <task>-merge.log for the
# merge validation checkout; a failure stops the run rather than letting the
# environment's problem be reported as the candidate's.
# worktree_prepare=npm ci --silent
# worktree_prepare_timeout_s=900
# Roles are configuration — see docs/configuration.md. Each role.* value is a
# comma-separated failover chain, primary first (e.g.
# role.implementer=codex,claude): a fallback engine only ever activates once
# it has passed `orchid plugins test <engine> <role>` (the capability
# suite) -- see `orchid doctor`'s per-role chain lines. Tested defaults
# (single entries here; add a fallback with a comma once it's been tested):
role.orchestrator=claude
role.implementer=codex
role.reviewer=agy
role.arbiter=claude
role.plan_critic=codex
# Read from the TARGET repository being driven (or ~/.orchid/config, or an
# ORCHID_PACK_BUDGET_BYTES env override) -- never from the orchid
# installation's own orchid.config. `orchid doctor` prints the resolved value
# and the layer it came from; a pack over this budget fails the LAUNCH
# (input_overflow) before any engine starts.
pack_budget_bytes=65536

# Custom roles (v1-m3 Task 7): a role.<id>= binding for any id that is NOT
# one of the five above resolves a role plugin (kind=role, shipped with a
# descriptor.role -- docs/specs/plugins.md) discovered on the same search
# path as engines; `orchid doctor` FAILs if the descriptor isn't
# discoverable. Every role.<id> binding, built-in or custom, additionally
# accepts a sibling role.<id>.blocking=false (default true) marking it
# non-blocking: a failed non-blocking role's job is journaled and the run
# continues rather than infra-failing (see docs/specs/operations.md).
# role.<id>=<engine>[,<engine>...]
# role.<id>.blocking=false
# Note: a custom <id> may use `-` or `_` interchangeably (both map to the
# same env-override variable, e.g. role.code-reviewer and role.code_reviewer
# both resolve ORCHID_ROLE_CODE_REVIEWER) -- never rely on `-` vs `_` to
# distinguish two otherwise-identical role ids; they collide.

# Dual review routing (v1-m2): review.<tier> is a comma-separated failover
# chain of engine names consulted for the risk-tiered reviewer slots (see
# docs/specs/kernel.md's Independence section) — low tier wants one
# engine-independent reviewer; medium/high want two (worktree-capable for
# depth + engine-independent for diversity).
#
# v1.1 (docs/specs/kernel.md, "Review depth"): at medium/high the depth half
# of that pairing is no longer only a preference. `orchid jobs review-plan`
# labels each slot `worktree` or `inline`, its depth pass searches past this
# chain into role.reviewer's chain and the implementer's own engine to find a
# worktree-capable slot, and `orchid drive` will not make a DETERMINISTIC
# approval at those tiers unless one of the counted reviews is credited to a
# `worktree` slot of the attempt's PINNED plan — it stops at an arbitrable
# `review-evidence` boundary instead. Credit follows the pin, so changing an
# engine's binding or capabilities after a review is filed neither withdraws
# that review's depth nor grants it any. Nothing here refuses a slot or an engine over depth: an
# install whose only reviewers are inline still gets every slot, and still
# reviews every task; what it does not get is an approval no human read.
# Built-in defaults shown here:
# review.low=agy
# review.medium=codex-review,agy
# review.high=codex-review,agy

# Everything below is commented with its built-in default — uncomment and
# change only what this repo needs. Full key reference: lib/config-keys.txt
# (also: `orchid config list` prints the EFFECTIVE value + provenance for
# every key, live).
# concurrency=2
# lock_break_s=900
# verb_lock_wait_s=10
# stall_minutes=10
# T040: the CPU floor of the stall check -- OPT-IN, off by default (0).
# stall_minutes catches a job that stops writing to its log; this catches
# one that keeps writing and stops working -- an engine still heartbeating
# while its cumulative CPU time barely moves. With a floor above zero, less
# than that many CPU-seconds across the last stall_minutes of heartbeats is
# reported (and killed) as `stalled`. Off by default because CPU alone
# cannot tell a dead engine from a healthy one blocked on a vendor API
# (F35 retracted the signal after a working job burned ~9 CPU-seconds in 40
# minutes); opt in only if you know your engines' CPU profile.
# cpu_stall_min_s=0
# timeout_minutes=60
# agy_max_bytes=100000
# v1-m4: same shape as agy_max_bytes above -- plugins/engines/hermes/run is
# ALSO an inline-only reviewer (capabilities=structured_text, no
# workspace_read; see docs/engines/hermes.md), so an oversized diff.patch
# fails closed the same way rather than getting the workspace-read engines'
# diff.stat relief below.
# hermes_max_bytes=100000
# spool_max_bytes=262144
# v1-m4: worktree-read review packs. A review/critique diff.patch larger
# than this many bytes is replaced with diff.stat (git diff --stat +
# --name-status) instead, but ONLY when the resolved reviewer/critic engine
# declares the workspace_read capability (it can read the checkout itself);
# an inline-only engine still gets the full diff.patch regardless of size,
# subject to the ordinary pack_budget_bytes overflow check.
# pack_diff_inline_max_bytes=262144
# v1-m3: cross-run lessons.md byte cap (docs/specs/kernel.md, Cross-run
# lessons). `orchid lessons consolidate` refuses rather than deleting an
# active lesson when hygiene alone can't fit under this cap.
# lessons_max_bytes=16384
# gc_older_than_s=86400
# infra_max=3
# v1.1: the rework budget -- how many `attempts` a task may consume before
# `orchid drive` stops retrying it and blocks it for a human. Was a
# hardcoded 3 in the driver, changeable nowhere; raise it here for a repo
# whose suites are slow to converge, or grant ONE task more rounds with
# `orchid task retry <id> --reason "..." --attempts N` (which records an
# `attempt_budget` in that task's frontmatter and journals the grant).
# rework_max=3
# v1.1: verification-failure classification. A FAILED verify is classified
# before it is charged, so the attempt budget keeps measuring the candidate
# rather than the harness. There is no signature list, and no way to declare a
# failure forgiven: four failures are waivable, and each is PROVED against the
# world rather than read out of the failure's wording --
#   - a package pin this repository's own freshness check REPORTS STALE, and
#   - an executable this candidate left mode 644 (stat'ing the files it added,
#     and the ones it modified whose base recorded mode 755, since a rewrite
#     that drops an exec bit is the same hand-off), and
#   - gitignored build state the integration checkout carries and the task's
#     worktree never received, since `git worktree add` reproduces only what
#     git TRACKS (lesson L003), and
#   - an assertion this repository ALREADY recorded as known-flaky, in a
#     register this candidate did not touch (flaky.quarantine, below).
# A run whose recorded exit status says it STOPPED SHORT (124/137/143) is
# REPORTED and still charged. It was a fifth verdict once, on the reading that
# the harness reaped a pass that therefore never spoke about the candidate --
# but that is equally the trailer a candidate which HUNG until a timeout reaped
# it leaves, and the one a suite that exits with the status deliberately
# leaves, and nothing in the log tells them apart.
# Each is proved against the world first, and then THIS failure must be
# attributable to that artifact: a failing line naming the file and refusing to
# execute it, naming it and calling it stale, or reporting that something the
# missing dependency tree PUBLISHES could not be resolved. Everything else
# charges, including a failure that merely coincides with an outstanding fault.
# A round is never waived as a round: one failing line nothing accounts for
# charges it, and the reason quotes that line.
# THE TWO ROUTES THAT READ A FILE OUT OF THIS REPOSITORY -- the pin check and
# the register -- read it only while it is an AUTHORITY ON THE CANDIDATE:
# untouched across base_sha..candidate_sha, tracked in candidate_sha, and
# present in the verified worktree with the same bytes and mode that commit
# records. An unstaged edit, a staged-and-uncommitted one, an untracked
# drop-in, a deletion or a mode change closes the route -- none of them is
# visible in a diff of two commits, and each is a file the implementer
# controls.
#
# The candidate-local package-pin freshness check that route RUNS, relative to
# the verified tree. Invoked only if it is a regular file there that states how
# it is run -- directly when it carries the exec bit, otherwise under the
# interpreter its own `#!` line names. Its nonzero EXIT is not enough: it must report
# a file stale in words, because a check that cannot find the formula or trips
# over metadata the candidate corrupted also exits nonzero and re-pinning fixes
# neither. Read only under the authority rule above, so a check the candidate
# changed -- or edited in the worktree without committing -- forgives nothing.
# Do not configure a whole-tree derived artifact here: every candidate would
# rewrite the same line and stale-base rebases would conflict (lesson L022).
# Orchid's Formula checksum is therefore release-gated, not a hand-off.
# `none` disables the route.
# Default: none
# handoff.pin_check=scripts/check-package-pin.sh --check
#
# The known-flaky register the `flaky` route reads, relative to the verified
# tree. One entry per line: `FLAKE: <literal substring of the failing line>`,
# optionally followed by ` -- <why>`; everything else in the file is prose the
# route ignores, so it can be the document a human actually reads. A signature
# is matched LITERALLY (never as a pattern) and must be at least 16 characters,
# so no entry can be written that matches everything, and it claims ONLY the
# lines it matches -- an aggregate `3 tests failed` alongside it is unexplained
# and charges the round.
# THE SAFETY IS NOT THE PATH, IT IS THE TIMING: a register THIS CANDIDATE
# CHANGED is not an authority on this candidate, so touching the file removes
# the route entirely. An implementer cannot quarantine the assertion it is
# failing -- and cannot reach around that by leaving the entry uncommitted in
# the worktree either, because the register is read only under the authority
# rule above. That question must also be ANSWERABLE -- a task record whose
# base_sha or candidate_sha is missing or does not resolve yields the same
# empty diff an untouched register does, and the route closes rather than
# reading that as permission. `none` disables the route.
# Default: tests/QUARANTINE.md
# flaky.quarantine=tests/QUARANTINE.md
# How many CONSECUTIVE rework rounds may come back with a byte-identical
# failure signature before the driver stops the loop and blocks the task for a
# human. An unchanged signature means the previous round's changes did not move
# the failure at all -- more attempts at that point re-ask a question already
# answered identically (dogfood finding F27). 0 disables the stop, leaving the
# ordinary attempt budget as the only backstop.
# rework_nonconvergence_max=3
# model=
# effort=medium
# rate_limit_backoff_s=3600
# engine_fail_threshold=3
# pump_stale_s=900
# v1-m4: `orchid service install` bakes this into the rendered launchd
# StartInterval (seconds) or the cron fallback's `*/N * * * *` line
# (floored to whole minutes there, minimum 1) -- change it and re-run
# `orchid service install` to take effect; editing this key alone does not
# touch an already-installed schedule.
# pump_interval_s=240
# How many pump passes one unchanged judgment boundary may wake an
# orchestrator for before the pump stops waking a model over it and the
# driver hands it to a human instead. "An orchestrator could settle this"
# is a static property of the boundary's kind, the task's status and the
# adapter's command surface; it cannot notice that the record has not moved
# in three passes. A run whose tasks are all `done` is the case this exists
# for: `orchid run accept` needs an operator's evidence, so no number of
# wakeups will ever move it.
# pump_wake_max=3
# v1-m2: high-risk arbitration's wait (seconds) for the preferred
# role.arbiter engine before a fallback arbiter may decide (PROTOCOL.md,
# HEADLESS OPERATION).
# arbiter_wait_s=14400

# v1.1: the OPERATOR HAND-OFF (PROTOCOL.md, THE TICK's implementing arm).
# Some candidates need mechanical work that requires EXECUTION -- applying a
# linter's own fix, setting the mode bit on a newly added executable, or
# running a candidate-local generator whose output is checked in -- and an
# engine profile that denies on the command STRING can perform none of it.
# Never use this pause to re-pin a whole-tree release checksum: that belongs
# on the integration branch at release time (see `handoff.pin_check` above).
# Set this to `required` when your implementer
# is such a profile: a drive pass then STOPS at an `operator-handoff` boundary
# after the implementer's envelope reconciles and BEFORE `orchid verify`,
# instead of verifying a candidate that was never going to pass and spending a
# rework round on it. Perform the steps, then record them with
# `orchid task handoff <id> --ack --reason "..."` -- the acknowledgement is
# bound to that task's current candidate_sha AND to the tree's HEAD, so a
# resumed session proceeds, while a rebase, a fresh rework round or one more
# commit after the ack invalidates it (INV-07; re-run the verb). `off` (the
# default) never gates anything; any OTHER value reads as `required`, so a
# typo here can only ever route more work to a human, never less. Turning it
# on is an operator decision, landed like any other config change with
# `orchid config commit --reason "..."`.
#
# A second arm asks for the same pause without being configured (INV-16),
# where the mechanical step cannot be ROUTED to the engine that built the
# candidate. It does not replace this key. What it closes on its own is the
# implementer that resolves to no installed manifest at all; its other
# outcome -- the engine declares no `shell` -- cannot arise for a candidate
# orchid dispatched, because roles/implementer.role requires
# `workspace_write,shell,git` and the role gate refuses such an engine before
# it can build one. So THIS key stays the only cover for the case no manifest
# shows: a profile that DECLARES `shell` and is still not granted it, which
# the shipped `claude` adapter is on its implement path. The two arms compose
# and never override -- either turns the pause on, neither turns the other
# off -- so an engine declaring `shell` can never clear a pause you asked for.
# handoff_before_verify=off

# NOTE (T024, dogfood F26): the OPERATOR PREREQUISITE is a different stop at
# the same point, and it has no config key at all. A task whose verification
# depends on a step taken OUTSIDE this repository -- canonically applying, to
# the database its suite runs against, the migration that task itself authored
# -- declares it per task at planning time (`orchid task set <id>
# operator_prerequisite "..."`), because only some tasks have one and a task
# that needs a migration applied needs it applied in every repository. See
# PROTOCOL.md's `testing` step.

# v1-m3: kernel-owned hook points (docs/specs/plugins.md, Hooks section).
# Each hook.<point> is an ordered, comma-separated list of plugin NAMEs (the
# short discovery name, e.g. "stubhook" -- a qualified id like "acme/stubhook"
# is not accepted in a binding in v1); `:required` marks a handler whose
# failure blocks the edge (exit 15) --
# an optional handler's failure is only journaled and skipped. No built-in
# defaults; an unbound point runs no handler at all.
# hook.after_plan_draft=
# hook.before_arbitration=
# hook.on_verify_fail=
# hook.before_merge=
# hook.on_blocker=
# hook_timeout_s=600

# v1-m4: `orchid init` installs a `.git/hooks/pre-push` guard (defense-in-
# depth against pushing orchid-managed refs -- task/* branches, the
# integration branch -- to origin; PROTOCOL.md already forbids it outright).
# Never overwrites a pre-existing pre-push hook of your own. Set to false to
# skip installing it.
# push_guard=true

# v1-m4: `orchid status --html` writes a self-contained static status page
# to this path (relative paths resolve under `.orchid/`, same as every
# other runtime/ path this file documents) -- the "check from another
# room" surface, regenerated best-effort at THE TICK's step 5. Never
# served; open the file directly.
# status_page=runtime/status.html

# v1-m4 Task 7: OpenClaw notify channel (docs/engines/openclaw.md). Both
# empty/unset by default -- no channel configured means `orchid notify`
# never writes an outbox file at all; BLOCKERS.md + terminal stays a
# complete surface on its own (docs/specs/operations.md). `notify.channel`
# is OpenClaw's OWN channel enum (telegram|whatsapp|discord|irc|
# googlechat|slack|signal|imessage|feishu|nostr|msteams|mattermost|
# nextcloud-talk|matrix|raft|line|zalo|clickclack|zalouser|sms|
# synology-chat|tlon|qqbot|twitch -- `openclaw message send --help`);
# `notify.to` is the recipient (`--target`: E.164, chat id, channelId, etc,
# per that channel). `notify.plugin` selects WHICH kind=notify plugin dir
# the pump's outbox drain launches (a plugin directory NAME under
# plugins/notify/, e.g. `hermes` -- never a manifest id), default openclaw.
# notify.plugin=openclaw
# notify.channel=
# notify.to=
# v1-m4 Task 7: setting this to anything turns inbox hardening ON for
# EVERY `orchid answer` call, not just ones that self-assert a sender --
# once configured, --nonce is mandatory for every caller (local terminal
# included; read it off BLOCKERS.md/the .question file), and a caller that
# additionally sets ORCHID_ANSWER_SENDER (a remote surface -- the openclaw
# AgentSkill, currently the only one) must also appear in this list.
# Leaving this unset/empty means no remote answer path exists at all, so
# `orchid answer` stays in its plain lenient local-terminal shape (no
# nonce needed) for everyone. Comma-separated exact sender identifiers.
# answer_allowlist=
# v1-m4 Task 7: a question (`.orchid/runtime/answers/<qid>.question`)
# expires this many seconds after it was minted (file mtime) -- `orchid
# answer` refuses an expired qid outright (die + journal note), regardless
# of sender.
# answer_expiry_s=86400
# v1-m4 Task 7: `runners/orchid-pump`'s outbox drain gives up on a queued
# notify-channel message after this many consecutive failed `send`
# attempts, quarantining it (`runtime/outbox/<qid>.reason-send-failed`)
# rather than retrying forever.
# send_retry_max=5
